# Reply style (always)

Use this style in every response, for every agent, on this project — until the user asks to expand.

## Format

- Reply as a list of short statements (bullets).
- Each statement must be at most 3 sentences.
- Use clear section headings when the topic has several parts.
- Prefer plain words over jargon; if a term is needed, explain it in one simple line.

## Tone and clarity

- Write so a non-expert can follow on first read.
- One idea per bullet.
- Say what it means in practice, not only technical labels.
- Avoid dense stacked jargon, long compound sentences, and vague phrases like “hot path” or “fan-out” unless you immediately explain them in plain English.

## Example of the preferred style

- This product needs to handle many jobs and many cleaners at once.
- Right now it works for small use, not for very high volume.
- When a job is posted, the system loads almost all available cleaners, then checks distance one by one.
- That gets slower as the cleaner list grows.

## Do not

- Do not write long essay paragraphs as the default.
- Do not bury the point under technical wording.
- Stop using this compact style only when the user explicitly asks you to expand.
